19
00:01:50,926 --> 00:01:55,897
Let's fix our notation, because shapes are everything in this chapter.

20
00:01:55,897 --> 00:02:00,726
Batch is how many sequences we process at once, two in the examples.

21
00:02:00,726 --> 00:02:05,484
Seq, or n, is the number of tokens, six for the cat sat on the mat.

22
00:02:05,484 --> 00:02:10,882
D model is the embedding width, seven hundred sixty eight for GPT two small.

23
00:02:10,882 --> 00:02:12,302
Num heads is twelve.

24
00:02:12,302 --> 00:02:18,551
D head, also called d k, is d model divided by the number of heads, which is sixty four.

25
00:02:18,551 --> 00:02:26,079
And d f f, the hidden size of the feed forward network, is four times d model, three thousand seventy two.

26
00:02:26,079 --> 00:02:32,542
Keep these numbers in your head: seven sixty eight, twelve, sixty four, three oh seven two.

27
00:02:33,391 --> 00:02:34,159
Section one.

28
00:02:34,159 --> 00:02:36,463
Why static embeddings aren't enough.

29
00:02:37,313 --> 00:02:39,715
In chapter nine we built embeddings.

30
00:02:39,715 --> 00:02:44,386
Each token ID looks up a row in a table, and we add a position vector.

31
00:02:44,386 --> 00:02:56,662
So in, I went to the river bank, and, I deposited money at the bank, the token bank, which is ID three three three one in GPT two's vocabulary, fetches exactly the same row both times.

32
00:02:56,662 --> 00:03:06,136
The position vectors differ a little because bank sits at a different position, but nothing in the embedding layer knows about river or money.

33
00:03:06,136 --> 00:03:07,404
It's a pure lookup.

34
00:03:07,404 --> 00:03:13,809
The meaning of bank depends on its neighbours, and the embedding layer can't see the neighbours.

35
00:03:14,659 --> 00:03:18,054
Here's the chapter nine forward pass as a reminder.

36
00:03:18,054 --> 00:03:21,848
Token embedding, shape batch by seq by seven sixty eight.

37
00:03:21,848 --> 00:03:23,912
Position embedding, same shape.

38
00:03:23,912 --> 00:03:24,511
Add them.

39
00:03:24,511 --> 00:03:33,164
So the input to this chapter is a tensor of shape batch, seq, seven sixty eight, which encodes what each token is and where it is.

40
00:03:33,164 --> 00:03:37,691
What's missing is what each token means in this particular sentence.

41
00:03:37,691 --> 00:03:39,555
That's the job of attention.

42
00:03:40,405 --> 00:03:42,574
A helpful analogy from the book.

43
00:03:42,574 --> 00:03:51,523
A static embedding is like a dictionary entry that lists every sense of bank at once: river edge, financial institution, to rely on.

44
00:03:51,523 --> 00:03:55,387
You get all of them blended and don't know which applies.

45
00:03:55,387 --> 00:04:01,691
Attention is like a smart reader who reads the whole sentence and highlights the right sense.

46
00:04:01,691 --> 00:04:06,504
In river bank, bank looks at river and moves towards the first meaning.

47
00:04:06,504 --> 00:04:10,911
In savings bank, it looks at savings and moves toward the second.

48
00:04:10,911 --> 00:04:13,351
Crucially, the shape doesn't change.

49
00:04:13,351 --> 00:04:18,029
Six by seven sixty eight goes in, six by seven sixty eight comes out.

50
00:04:18,029 --> 00:04:19,588
Only the values change.

51
00:04:20,438 --> 00:04:29,993
I wanted to see this for real, so I ran the pretrained GPT two model on four sentences and took the hidden vector for bank at every layer.

52
00:04:29,993 --> 00:04:33,040
Then I compared them with cosine similarity.

53
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:39,756
At layer zero, before any attention, they're nearly identical, around zero point nine nine eight.

54
00:04:39,756 --> 00:04:52,358
As we go up the layers, the pink line, river bank versus money bank, drops to about zero point seven five, while the gold line, two financial uses, stays around zero point nine five.

55
00:04:52,358 --> 00:04:56,027
So the representation genuinely separates by meaning.

56
00:04:56,027 --> 00:05:04,059
The jump back up at the last layer is a known quirk of GPT two's final layer, where all vectors become very similar.

57
00:05:04,059 --> 00:05:08,283
The lesson: attention layers are where context gets mixed in.

58
00:05:09,133 --> 00:05:12,379
And it's not only about words with two meanings.

59
00:05:12,379 --> 00:05:20,359
Attention handles coreference: in Alice went to the store, she bought milk, the word she needs information from Alice.

60
00:05:20,359 --> 00:05:28,609
Long-range agreement: in the keys to the cabinet are, the verb are must agree with keys, not with the nearer word cabinet.

61
00:05:28,609 --> 00:05:34,831
Syntax: in the cat that chased the mouse sat, the subject of sat is cat, several words back.

62
00:05:34,831 --> 00:05:38,753
Semantics: capital should pull in information about Paris.

63
00:05:38,753 --> 00:05:44,028
All of these are one mechanism: tokens exchanging information with each other.

64
00:05:44,878 --> 00:05:46,752
Here is the whole pipeline.

65
00:05:46,752 --> 00:05:52,720
Text goes into the tokenizer from chapter eight, giving token IDs of shape one by six.

66
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:59,798
The embedding layer from chapter nine turns them into static vectors, one by six by seven sixty eight.

67
00:05:59,798 --> 00:06:06,668
This chapter adds the transformer block, which keeps the shape but makes the vectors context aware.

68
00:06:06,668 --> 00:06:19,923
Chapter eleven stacks twelve of these blocks and adds the language model head, which produces logits over all fifty thousand two hundred fifty seven tokens, and then we sample the next token.

69
00:06:20,773 --> 00:06:21,551
Section two.

70
00:06:21,551 --> 00:06:22,717
What is attention?

71
00:06:22,717 --> 00:06:25,893
Three analogies, then the maths in plain English.

72
00:06:26,743 --> 00:06:28,922
First analogy, a committee meeting.

73
00:06:28,922 --> 00:06:30,415
Every token is a member.

74
00:06:30,415 --> 00:06:34,711
When it's sat's turn, it first listens to everyone, including itself.

75
00:06:34,711 --> 00:06:37,387
Then it decides how relevant each input is.

76
00:06:37,387 --> 00:06:40,437
Cat is very relevant, because it answers who sat.

77
00:06:40,437 --> 00:06:42,927
On is somewhat relevant, it hints where.

78
00:06:42,927 --> 00:06:44,359
The is barely relevant.

79
00:06:44,359 --> 00:06:49,837
Finally it updates its own position by blending everyone's input, weighted by relevance.

80
00:06:49,837 --> 00:06:56,311
After this, the vector for sat encodes something like, an action performed by the cat, at some location.

81
00:06:57,161 --> 00:06:59,497
Second analogy, a library search.

82
00:06:59,497 --> 00:07:01,620
Your search text is the query.

83
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:04,168
Each book has index terms, its keys.

84
00:07:04,168 --> 00:07:06,858
And each book has contents, its value.

85
00:07:06,858 --> 00:07:19,669
The search compares your query to every key, books tagged animals, pets, felines score high, books on history or dogs score low, and you get back the contents weighted by relevance.

86
00:07:19,669 --> 00:07:21,510
Attention is exactly this.

87
00:07:21,510 --> 00:07:32,056
Each token produces a query, what am I looking for, a key, how can I be found, and a value, what information do I hand over if someone attends to me.

88
00:07:32,906 --> 00:07:35,828
Why three different vectors instead of one?

89
00:07:35,828 --> 00:07:38,477
Because the roles really are different.

90
00:07:38,477 --> 00:07:46,495
The way cat advertises itself, its key, might say, I'm a subject noun, which attracts verbs looking for their subject.

91
00:07:46,495 --> 00:07:52,610
But what cat actually hands over, its value, is its semantic content, animal, feline, pet.

92
00:07:52,610 --> 00:08:00,151
Separating them lets the model learn what to look for, what to be found by, and what to pass on, independently.

93
00:08:00,151 --> 00:08:05,519
And each one is simply a learned linear projection of the same input embedding.

94
00:08:06,369 --> 00:08:08,232
Third analogy, a spotlight.

95
00:08:08,232 --> 00:08:14,098
Each token shines a spotlight over all the tokens, with different brightness on each.

96
00:08:14,098 --> 00:08:18,859
These brightnesses are the attention weights, and they add up to one.

97
00:08:18,859 --> 00:08:25,622
For sat, maybe seventy percent on cat, twenty percent on on, and ten percent spread over the rest.

98
00:08:25,622 --> 00:08:35,904
The new representation of sat is then zero point seven times cat's value, plus zero point two times on's value, plus zero point one times the others.

99
00:08:35,904 --> 00:08:41,563
These numbers are illustrative, but the formula is exactly what the code computes.

100
00:08:42,413 --> 00:08:44,622
Now the maths in plain words.

101
00:08:44,622 --> 00:08:48,126
Start with X, six tokens by seven sixty eight.

102
00:08:48,126 --> 00:08:54,905
Step one, project X with three weight matrices to get Q, K and V, each six by sixty four.

103
00:08:54,905 --> 00:08:59,932
Step two, score every pair: the dot product of query i with key j.

104
00:08:59,932 --> 00:09:02,217
That gives a six by six table.

105
00:09:02,217 --> 00:09:07,396
Step three, divide by the square root of sixty four, which is eight.

106
00:09:07,396 --> 00:09:13,490
Step four, apply softmax to each row so the weights are positive and sum to one.

107
00:09:13,490 --> 00:09:18,060
Step five, each output is the weighted sum of value vectors.

108
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:25,372
All of that compresses into the famous formula: softmax of Q K transpose over root d k, times V.

109
00:09:26,222 --> 00:09:28,592
Here's the same thing as a diagram.

110
00:09:28,592 --> 00:09:32,723
X, six by seven sixty eight, branches into three projections.

111
00:09:32,723 --> 00:09:35,500
Each produces a six by sixty four matrix.

112
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:40,579
Q and K meet to form the six by six score table, scaled by root sixty four.

113
00:09:40,579 --> 00:09:44,371
That goes through masking and softmax, still six by six.

114
00:09:44,371 --> 00:09:48,840
Then the weights multiply V to give the output, six by sixty four.

115
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:52,159
Notice V doesn't take part in the scoring at all.

116
00:09:52,159 --> 00:09:54,190
It only gets mixed at the end.

117
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:55,913
One more term.

118
00:09:55,913 --> 00:10:04,083
What we're building is self-attention: queries, keys and values all come from the same sequence, so the sentence attends to itself.

119
00:10:04,083 --> 00:10:16,119
The original transformer for translation also used cross-attention, where the decoder's queries look at the encoder's keys and values, which come from a different sentence, the source language.

120
00:10:16,119 --> 00:10:22,667
GPT is a decoder only model, so we only need self-attention, with a causal mask, which we'll add shortly.

121
00:10:23,517 --> 00:10:24,467
Section three.

122
00:10:24,467 --> 00:10:28,744
Building self-attention, step by step, every line, every shape.

123
00:10:29,594 --> 00:10:31,070
Step zero, the setup.

124
00:10:31,070 --> 00:10:40,492
We import torch, the neural network module nn, the functional module F, which gives us softmax and gelu, and math for the square root.

125
00:10:40,492 --> 00:10:49,001
Then we fake the output of chapter nine: torch dot randn creates random numbers of shape two by six by seven sixty eight.

126
00:10:49,001 --> 00:10:53,782
Two sentences, six tokens each, seven sixty eight numbers per token.

127
00:10:53,782 --> 00:10:58,985
When I ran it, the print confirmed torch size two, six, seven sixty eight.

128
00:10:58,985 --> 00:11:06,580
In a real model these would be the embeddings, here they're random because we only care about the mechanics.

129
00:11:07,430 --> 00:11:09,306
Step one, the projections.

130
00:11:09,306 --> 00:11:12,771
d model is seven sixty eight, d k is sixty four.

131
00:11:12,771 --> 00:11:18,835
We create three linear layers, each mapping seven sixty eight numbers to sixty four.

132
00:11:18,835 --> 00:11:26,198
Bias equals false, in GPT two style, because the layer norm before attention already handles centring.

133
00:11:26,198 --> 00:11:28,869
Then we apply each to the embeddings.

134
00:11:28,869 --> 00:11:37,243
A linear layer acts on the last dimension only, so two by six by seven sixty eight becomes two by six by sixty four.

135
00:11:37,243 --> 00:11:44,606
Every one of the twelve tokens across both sentences now has a sixty four number query, key and value.

136
00:11:44,606 --> 00:11:48,648
These three weight matrices are learned during training.

137
00:11:48,648 --> 00:11:50,453
Right now they're random.

138
00:11:51,303 --> 00:11:55,114
A quick zoom into nn dot Linear, since it's everywhere.

139
00:11:55,114 --> 00:12:06,132
Linear seven sixty eight to sixty four stores a weight matrix of shape sixty four by seven sixty eight, and computes x times W transpose on the last dimension.

140
00:12:06,132 --> 00:12:11,122
All the leading dimensions, batch and seq, just come along for the ride.

141
00:12:11,122 --> 00:12:18,329
So two by six by seven sixty eight times seven sixty eight by sixty four gives two by six by sixty four.

142
00:12:18,329 --> 00:12:26,506
Each projection has seven sixty eight times sixty four, which is forty nine thousand one hundred fifty two parameters.

143
00:12:26,506 --> 00:12:27,545
Why sixty four?

144
00:12:27,545 --> 00:12:33,644
It's what each head will get later, when we split seven sixty eight across twelve heads.

145
00:12:34,494 --> 00:12:36,114
Step two, the scores.

146
00:12:36,114 --> 00:12:40,283
We want the dot product of every query with every key.

147
00:12:40,283 --> 00:12:50,703
For matrix multiplication the inner dimensions must match, so we transpose K's last two dimensions with transpose minus two, minus one.

148
00:12:50,703 --> 00:12:55,720
K goes from two by six by sixty four to two by sixty four by six.

149
00:12:55,720 --> 00:13:03,593
Now Q, two by six by sixty four, times K transpose, two by sixty four by six, gives two by six by six.

150
00:13:03,593 --> 00:13:08,225
The leading two is a batch dimension, handled automatically.

151
00:13:08,225 --> 00:13:13,165
Entry i j of the six by six matrix is query i dotted with key j.

152
00:13:14,015 --> 00:13:15,934
How do we read this matrix?

153
00:13:15,934 --> 00:13:19,204
Rows are queries, the token doing the looking.

154
00:13:19,204 --> 00:13:22,331
Columns are keys, the token being looked at.

155
00:13:22,331 --> 00:13:28,941
So scores zero, two, four is how strongly token two matches token four in the first sentence.

156
00:13:28,941 --> 00:13:38,324
A dot product is the sum of sixty four element-wise products, large and positive when the query and key point in similar directions.

157
00:13:38,324 --> 00:13:51,260
Here's the real first row from my notebook run: minus two point six, two point four nine, minus four point four two, minus zero point one seven, two point six, three point nine four.

158
00:13:51,260 --> 00:13:57,870
These are spread out over about plus or minus five, and that's a problem the next step fixes.

159
00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:01,131
Let's do one completely by hand.

160
00:14:01,131 --> 00:14:07,008
This is exercise one from the notebook, and I checked every number in PyTorch.

161
00:14:07,008 --> 00:14:09,193
Two tokens, three dimensions.

162
00:14:09,193 --> 00:14:11,378
Query zero is one, zero, one.

163
00:14:11,378 --> 00:14:13,413
Key zero is one, one, zero.

164
00:14:13,413 --> 00:14:15,296
Their dot product is one.

165
00:14:15,296 --> 00:14:19,139
Query zero with key one, zero one one, is also one.

166
00:14:19,139 --> 00:14:24,639
Query one, zero one one, with key zero gives one, with key one gives two.

167
00:14:24,639 --> 00:14:33,229
Divide by root three, about one point seven three, giving zero point five seven seven and one point one five five.

168
00:14:33,229 --> 00:14:36,996
Softmax of two equal numbers is a half and a half.

169
00:14:36,996 --> 00:14:45,661
Softmax of zero point five seven seven and one point one five five is zero point three six and zero point six four.

170
00:14:45,661 --> 00:14:55,757
Finally mix the values: row zero is half of two, zero, one plus half of one, two, zero, which is one point five, one, zero point five.

171
00:14:55,757 --> 00:15:08,340
Row one is zero point three six times the first value plus zero point six four times the second, giving one point three six, one point two eight, zero point three six.

172
00:15:09,190 --> 00:15:10,563
Step three, scaling.

173
00:15:10,563 --> 00:15:14,271
One line: divide the scores by the square root of d k.

174
00:15:14,271 --> 00:15:18,047
With d k equal to sixty four, that's dividing by eight.

175
00:15:18,047 --> 00:15:20,382
The shape stays two by six by six.

176
00:15:20,382 --> 00:15:22,030
But why the square root?

177
00:15:22,030 --> 00:15:29,308
Let's actually look at the maths, because the notebook just says a proof shows this keeps variance stable.

178
00:15:30,158 --> 00:15:31,611
Here's the argument.

179
00:15:31,611 --> 00:15:37,350
Suppose the entries of q and k are independent with mean zero and variance one.

180
00:15:37,350 --> 00:15:46,649
The dot product is a sum of d products, each with variance one, so the total variance is d and the standard deviation is root d.

181
00:15:46,649 --> 00:15:50,136
I measured it with twenty thousand random pairs.

182
00:15:50,136 --> 00:15:56,965
At d equals sixty four the standard deviation was seven point nine four, almost exactly eight.

183
00:15:56,965 --> 00:16:00,380
At seven sixty eight it was about twenty eight.

184
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:06,119
Divide by root d, and the green line sits right at one regardless of dimension.

185
00:16:06,119 --> 00:16:14,184
That's the whole justification: scaling keeps the scores at a consistent size no matter how wide the heads are.

186
00:16:15,034 --> 00:16:17,258
And why do large scores matter?

187
00:16:17,258 --> 00:16:20,128
Because softmax exaggerates differences.

188
00:16:20,128 --> 00:16:27,806
Scores of one, two and three give weights of about nine percent, twenty four percent and sixty six percent.

189
00:16:27,806 --> 00:16:37,637
Multiply those scores by eight, roughly what happens without scaling at d equals sixty four, and you get essentially zero, zero, and one.

190
00:16:37,637 --> 00:16:40,723
All the attention collapses onto one token.

191
00:16:40,723 --> 00:16:49,046
Worse, in that saturated regime the gradients of softmax are nearly zero, so the query and key weights barely learn.

192
00:16:49,046 --> 00:16:52,276
Scaling keeps softmax in its sensitive range.

193
00:16:53,126 --> 00:16:54,574
Step four, softmax.

194
00:16:54,574 --> 00:17:01,128
F dot softmax with dim equals minus one normalises along the last dimension, the keys.

195
00:17:01,128 --> 00:17:06,692
So each row, one query, becomes a probability distribution over the keys.

196
00:17:06,692 --> 00:17:20,868
In my run, token zero's weights were zero point one one, zero point two oh, zero point oh nine, zero point one five, zero point two one, zero point two five, and they sum to exactly one.

197
00:17:20,868 --> 00:17:23,612
The dim argument matters enormously.

198
00:17:23,612 --> 00:17:29,480
Softmax over dim zero would normalise across the batch, which is meaningless.

199
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:31,157
Always minus one here.

200
00:17:32,007 --> 00:17:33,973
Step five, mix the values.

201
00:17:33,973 --> 00:17:39,341
Attention weights are two by six by six, V is two by six by sixty four.

202
00:17:39,341 --> 00:17:42,819
Multiply, and we get two by six by sixty four.

203
00:17:42,819 --> 00:17:48,867
Look at a single sentence: a six by six matrix times a six by sixty four matrix.

204
00:17:48,867 --> 00:17:53,857
Row i of the result is the sum over j of weight i j times value j.

205
00:17:53,857 --> 00:17:56,579
A weighted average of value vectors.

206
00:17:56,579 --> 00:18:07,845
If token two gave seventy percent to token one and twenty percent to token three, its output is mostly token one's value, with some of token three's.

207
00:18:07,845 --> 00:18:13,516
Each token's output now carries information from the tokens it attended to.

208
00:18:14,366 --> 00:18:20,603
Here are those steps on real numbers from a run I did with the tokens of the cat sat on the mat.

209
00:18:20,603 --> 00:18:23,916
Left: raw scores, spanning about plus or minus six.

210
00:18:23,916 --> 00:18:28,919
Middle: the same after dividing by eight, now within about plus or minus one.

211
00:18:28,919 --> 00:18:30,283
Right: after softmax.

212
00:18:30,283 --> 00:18:38,079
Every row sums to one, and with random untrained weights the attention is fairly spread out, which is what you'd expect.

213
00:18:38,079 --> 00:18:41,523
Training is what will make these patterns meaningful.

214
00:18:42,373 --> 00:18:47,773
Now we package everything into one function, scaled dot product attention.

215
00:18:47,773 --> 00:18:50,618
It takes Q, K, V, and an optional mask.

216
00:18:50,618 --> 00:18:57,113
Line one reads d k from the last dimension of Q, so the function works for any head size.

217
00:18:57,113 --> 00:19:01,637
Then scores equals Q times K transpose, shape batch, seq, seq.

218
00:19:01,637 --> 00:19:03,023
Divide by root d k.

219
00:19:03,023 --> 00:19:10,247
If a mask is provided, masked fill replaces every score where the mask is zero with minus infinity.

220
00:19:10,247 --> 00:19:12,144
We'll see why in a moment.

221
00:19:12,144 --> 00:19:15,866
Softmax along the last dimension gives the weights.

222
00:19:15,866 --> 00:19:23,528
Multiply by V for the output, and return both the output and the weights, so we can visualise them later.

223
00:19:23,528 --> 00:19:25,717
About ten lines of real logic.

224
00:19:25,717 --> 00:19:28,490
This is the core of every transformer.

225
00:19:29,340 --> 00:19:32,038
Here is the shape table to memorise.

226
00:19:32,038 --> 00:19:36,537
Projection: two six seven sixty eight to two six sixty four.

227
00:19:36,537 --> 00:19:38,936
Transpose K: two sixty four six.

228
00:19:38,936 --> 00:19:41,410
Q times K transpose: two six six.

229
00:19:41,410 --> 00:19:44,258
Scale, mask, softmax keep two six six.

230
00:19:44,258 --> 00:19:47,557
Weights times V: back to two six sixty four.

231
00:19:47,557 --> 00:19:58,278
And the three classic bugs: forgetting the transpose, which crashes with a shape error, or worse, silently works when d k happens to equal seq.

232
00:19:58,278 --> 00:20:00,602
Softmax on the wrong dimension.

233
00:20:00,602 --> 00:20:04,125
And a mask with a shape that doesn't broadcast.

234
00:20:04,125 --> 00:20:07,499
When learning, print shapes after every line.

235
00:20:08,349 --> 00:20:09,242
Section four.

236
00:20:09,242 --> 00:20:12,061
Causal masking, no peeking at the future.

237
00:20:12,911 --> 00:20:14,377
Why do we need a mask?

238
00:20:14,377 --> 00:20:18,311
In training, one sentence gives us several lessons at once.

239
00:20:18,311 --> 00:20:19,844
After the, predict cat.

240
00:20:19,844 --> 00:20:22,377
After the cat, predict sat, and so on.

241
00:20:22,377 --> 00:20:25,044
All positions are processed in parallel.

242
00:20:25,044 --> 00:20:29,444
But the attention we built lets every token see every other token.

243
00:20:29,444 --> 00:20:36,577
So while the model is supposed to predict what comes after cat, cat can attend to sat, which is the answer.

244
00:20:36,577 --> 00:20:41,710
In this illustrative row, more than half the attention goes to future tokens.

245
00:20:41,710 --> 00:20:45,710
That's like sitting an exam with the answer key on the desk.

246
00:20:45,710 --> 00:20:52,777
The model learns to copy, and then fails completely at generation time, when the future doesn't exist yet.

247
00:20:53,627 --> 00:20:54,882
The rule is simple.

248
00:20:54,882 --> 00:21:01,622
The token at position i can attend to positions zero through i, itself and the past, never the future.

249
00:21:01,622 --> 00:21:05,257
So sat at position two can look at the, cat and itself.

250
00:21:05,257 --> 00:21:12,063
If you draw allowed cells in a grid with queries as rows and keys as columns, you get a lower triangle.

251
00:21:12,063 --> 00:21:19,398
The book's analogy: reading a mystery novel, you can remember every page you've read, but you can't flip ahead.

252
00:21:20,248 --> 00:21:22,521
Building the mask is one line.

253
00:21:22,521 --> 00:21:33,356
Torch dot ones makes a six by six matrix of ones, and torch dot tril keeps the lower triangle including the diagonal, zeroing everything above.

254
00:21:33,356 --> 00:21:35,175
This is the real output.

255
00:21:35,175 --> 00:21:39,342
Row zero has a single one: token zero sees only itself.

256
00:21:39,342 --> 00:21:43,055
Row two has three ones: tokens zero, one and two.

257
00:21:43,055 --> 00:21:47,071
Row five is all ones: the last token sees everything.

258
00:21:47,071 --> 00:21:54,497
One small note: the docstring calls this a boolean tensor, but it's actually float ones and zeros.

259
00:21:54,497 --> 00:22:00,028
That works fine because the attention function compares mask equals zero.

260
00:22:00,878 --> 00:22:01,915
Now we use it.

261
00:22:01,915 --> 00:22:10,355
Random Q, K and V of shape two by six by sixty four, the mask, and call our function with mask equals causal mask.

262
00:22:10,355 --> 00:22:16,130
Inside, masked fill sets every blocked score to minus infinity before softmax.

263
00:22:16,130 --> 00:22:26,124
Since e to the minus infinity is zero, those positions get exactly zero weight, and the remaining weights in each row still sum to one.

264
00:22:26,124 --> 00:22:27,753
Here's my real output.

265
00:22:27,753 --> 00:22:32,047
Token zero puts all its weight, one point zero, on itself.

266
00:22:32,047 --> 00:22:35,897
Token one splits zero point four and zero point six.

267
00:22:35,897 --> 00:22:38,636
Token two splits across three tokens.

268
00:22:38,636 --> 00:22:41,524
And the upper triangle is exactly zero.

269
00:22:41,524 --> 00:22:47,891
Notice also that the six by six mask broadcasts automatically across the batch of two.

270
00:22:48,741 --> 00:22:52,115
Here is the masking pipeline visually, from my run.

271
00:22:52,115 --> 00:22:55,424
On the left, the mask, ones in the lower triangle.

272
00:22:55,424 --> 00:23:01,246
In the middle, the scaled scores after masked fill: every future cell is minus infinity.

273
00:23:01,246 --> 00:23:07,731
On the right, after softmax: the first row is a single one, and every future cell is exactly zero.

274
00:23:07,731 --> 00:23:10,179
Why minus infinity and not just zero?

275
00:23:10,179 --> 00:23:15,935
Because a score of zero still gets e to the zero, which is one, a real chunk of weight.

276
00:23:15,935 --> 00:23:18,714
Only minus infinity gives exactly nothing.

277
00:23:19,564 --> 00:23:22,730
Let's be clear about training versus inference.

278
00:23:22,730 --> 00:23:32,024
In training we feed the whole sequence at once for efficiency, so the future tokens are physically present and must be hidden by the mask.

279
00:23:32,024 --> 00:23:40,038
During generation we produce one token at a time, so the future simply doesn't exist, and the restriction is automatic.

280
00:23:40,038 --> 00:23:44,820
The mask is what makes training conditions match generation conditions.

281
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:51,622
That's why a decoder model like GPT can learn from every position of a sentence in one parallel pass.

282
00:23:52,472 --> 00:23:53,423
Section five.

283
00:23:53,423 --> 00:23:55,032
From one head to many.

284
00:23:55,882 --> 00:24:02,048
A single head has one set of attention weights per token, one probability distribution.

285
00:24:02,048 --> 00:24:05,521
But language has many simultaneous relationships.

286
00:24:05,521 --> 00:24:14,167
Subject verb agreement, coreference, syntax, semantic links, and plain positional patterns like look at the previous word.

287
00:24:14,167 --> 00:24:17,498
One distribution can't do all of those at once.

288
00:24:17,498 --> 00:24:23,238
If it focuses on the subject, it can't simultaneously focus on the previous word.

289
00:24:23,238 --> 00:24:29,050
So we run several attention heads in parallel, each free to learn its own pattern.

290
00:24:29,899 --> 00:24:34,540
The neat part is that multi-head attention costs no extra parameters.

291
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:41,735
Instead of one head with seven sixty eight dimensions, we use twelve heads with sixty four dimensions each.

292
00:24:41,735 --> 00:24:44,761
Twelve times sixty four is seven sixty eight.

293
00:24:44,761 --> 00:24:53,033
Each head gets its own slice of Q, K and V, computes its own attention pattern, and produces a sixty four dimension output.

294
00:24:53,033 --> 00:24:59,959
Concatenate the twelve and you're back at seven sixty eight, and a final output projection blends them.

295
00:24:59,959 --> 00:25:10,853
I verified that the attention module has two million three hundred fifty nine thousand two hundred ninety six parameters whether you configure one head or twelve.

296
00:25:11,703 --> 00:25:13,208
Here's the constructor.

297
00:25:13,208 --> 00:25:20,927
The assert checks that d model divides evenly by the number of heads, seven sixty eight by twelve is sixty four, fine.

298
00:25:20,927 --> 00:25:21,973
We store d head.

299
00:25:21,973 --> 00:25:38,719
Then the first efficiency trick: instead of three separate linear layers, one linear layer maps seven sixty eight to three times seven sixty eight, two thousand three hundred and four outputs, producing Q, K and V for all heads in a single matrix multiply.

300
00:25:38,719 --> 00:25:45,849
Then an output projection, seven sixty eight to seven sixty eight, which mixes the heads after concatenation.

301
00:25:45,849 --> 00:25:50,951
And a dropout layer that we'll apply to the attention weights during training.

302
00:25:51,801 --> 00:25:53,684
The forward pass, part one.

303
00:25:53,684 --> 00:25:56,265
Unpack batch, seq and d model from x.

304
00:25:56,265 --> 00:26:03,171
Project: two by six by seven sixty eight becomes two by six by two thousand three hundred and four.

305
00:26:03,171 --> 00:26:04,705
Now the reshape trick.

306
00:26:04,705 --> 00:26:16,284
Reshape the last dimension, two thousand three hundred four, into three by twelve by sixty four: which of Q, K, V, which head, and which of the sixty four dimensions.

307
00:26:16,284 --> 00:26:21,167
Then permute the axes into the order three, batch, heads, seq, d head.

308
00:26:21,167 --> 00:26:29,049
That's what the indices two, zero, three, one, four mean: take old axis two first, then old axis zero, and so on.

309
00:26:29,049 --> 00:26:36,234
Finally index zero, one, two to split out Q, K and V, each of shape two by twelve by six by sixty four.

310
00:26:36,234 --> 00:26:40,349
Heads now sit next to batch, like an extra batch dimension.

311
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:46,328
Let's slow down on this trick, because it's the hardest part of the chapter.

312
00:26:46,328 --> 00:26:57,125
After the projection, each token's two thousand three hundred four numbers are laid out as all the Q's for the twelve heads, then all the K's, then all the V's.

313
00:26:57,125 --> 00:27:04,009
Reshape doesn't move any data, it just labels each number: this one is Q, head five, dimension thirty.

314
00:27:04,009 --> 00:27:09,407
Permute then rearranges the axes so the head axis sits before the sequence axis.

315
00:27:09,407 --> 00:27:15,413
The result: for each of the two sentences, twelve independent six by sixty four problems.

316
00:27:15,413 --> 00:27:27,695
And since matrix multiply in PyTorch only works on the last two dimensions and broadcasts over the rest, Q times K transpose computes all twenty four attention maps in a single call.

317
00:27:27,695 --> 00:27:29,450
No Python loop over heads.

318
00:27:30,299 --> 00:27:35,470
Part two is the same five steps as before, just with an extra head axis.

319
00:27:35,470 --> 00:27:41,574
Scores are now two by twelve by six by six: a six by six table per head per sentence.

320
00:27:41,574 --> 00:27:43,369
Scale by root sixty four.

321
00:27:43,369 --> 00:27:53,063
For the mask, if it's two dimensional, we unsqueeze twice to make it one by one by six by six, so it broadcasts across batch and heads.

322
00:27:53,063 --> 00:28:05,415
Masked fill with minus infinity, softmax on the last dimension, then dropout on the attention weights, which randomly zeroes some weights during training as regularisation.

323
00:28:05,415 --> 00:28:09,867
Then weights times V gives two by twelve by six by sixty four.

324
00:28:10,717 --> 00:28:12,731
Part three undoes the split.

325
00:28:12,731 --> 00:28:19,996
Transpose axes one and two, so we have batch, seq, heads, d head: two by six by twelve by sixty four.

326
00:28:19,996 --> 00:28:24,816
Now for each token, the twelve head outputs sit next to each other.

327
00:28:24,816 --> 00:28:35,390
Reshape to two by six by seven sixty eight, which concatenates the twelve sixty four dimension outputs into one seven sixty eight vector per token.

328
00:28:35,390 --> 00:28:39,778
Finally the output projection mixes information across heads.

329
00:28:39,778 --> 00:28:43,159
We return the output and the attention weights.

330
00:28:44,009 --> 00:28:50,086
Testing it: twelve heads, random input of two by six by seven sixty eight, a causal mask.

331
00:28:50,086 --> 00:29:02,512
My real output: input two six seven sixty eight, output two six seven sixty eight, and attention weights two by twelve by six by six, one six by six map for each of the twelve heads.

332
00:29:02,512 --> 00:29:09,886
The fact that the output shape equals the input shape is what lets us stack these layers as deep as we like.

333
00:29:10,736 --> 00:29:14,004
A practical PyTorch detail the book highlights.

334
00:29:14,004 --> 00:29:22,973
Transpose and permute don't actually move numbers in memory, they just change the strides, the recipe for walking through memory.

335
00:29:22,973 --> 00:29:25,476
The result is called non contiguous.

336
00:29:25,476 --> 00:29:31,733
View requires contiguous memory, so calling view after a transpose throws a runtime error.

337
00:29:31,733 --> 00:29:37,851
Reshape handles both cases: it returns a view when it can and makes a copy when it must.

338
00:29:37,851 --> 00:29:40,632
The alternative is contiguous then view.

339
00:29:40,632 --> 00:29:44,456
Rule of thumb: after transpose or permute, use reshape.

340
00:29:44,456 --> 00:29:50,922
The small snippet here is mine, you can paste it into the notebook to see the error yourself.

341
00:29:51,772 --> 00:29:52,635
Section six.

342
00:29:52,635 --> 00:29:54,865
The complete transformer block.

343
00:29:55,715 --> 00:29:57,755
Attention alone isn't enough.

344
00:29:57,755 --> 00:30:02,255
First, it's mostly linear: projections, then a weighted average.

345
00:30:02,255 --> 00:30:10,553
Stacking linear operations just gives another linear operation, so we need a non-linearity to learn complex functions.

346
00:30:10,553 --> 00:30:16,812
Second, deep stacks are hard to train, so we need residual connections and normalisation.

347
00:30:16,812 --> 00:30:24,829
Third, attention moves information between tokens, but we also want to process that information inside each token.

348
00:30:24,829 --> 00:30:35,518
The book's picture is an assembly line: each station adds something, but the original item always travels alongside, thanks to the residual connections.

349
00:30:36,367 --> 00:30:38,271
The feed forward network.

350
00:30:38,271 --> 00:30:39,642
Two linear layers.

351
00:30:39,642 --> 00:30:45,583
fc1 expands seven sixty eight to three thousand seventy two, four times wider.

352
00:30:45,583 --> 00:30:47,791
Then GELU, the non-linearity.

353
00:30:47,791 --> 00:30:48,781
Then dropout.

354
00:30:48,781 --> 00:30:52,513
Then fc2 projects back down to seven sixty eight.

355
00:30:52,513 --> 00:30:59,444
It's called position wise because the same network is applied independently to every token.

356
00:30:59,444 --> 00:31:05,460
Position zero gets FFN of x zero, position one gets FFN of x one, same weights.

357
00:31:05,460 --> 00:31:08,583
No information moves between tokens here.

358
00:31:08,583 --> 00:31:10,334
That's attention's job.

359
00:31:10,334 --> 00:31:20,159
In my run the shapes went two six seven sixty eight, to two six three oh seven two inside, and back to two six seven sixty eight.

360
00:31:21,009 --> 00:31:29,999
GELU, the Gaussian error linear unit, is x times phi of x, where phi is the cumulative distribution of a standard normal.

361
00:31:29,999 --> 00:31:35,125
Compared to ReLU, which is a hard zero for negatives, GELU is smooth.

362
00:31:35,125 --> 00:31:45,081
At x equals minus one it outputs about minus zero point one six instead of zero, and at plus one it gives about zero point eight four.

363
00:31:45,081 --> 00:31:50,950
The smooth curve gives gradients everywhere, which tends to train a bit better.

364
00:31:50,950 --> 00:31:54,219
GPT two, BERT, and most transformers use it.

365
00:31:55,069 --> 00:31:55,872
Parameters.

366
00:31:55,872 --> 00:32:05,652
fc1 has seven sixty eight times three oh seven two weights, about two point three six million, plus three thousand seventy two biases.

367
00:32:05,652 --> 00:32:10,469
fc2 is the same size the other way, plus seven sixty eight biases.

368
00:32:10,469 --> 00:32:16,162
Total about four point seven two million, roughly two thirds of a whole block.

369
00:32:16,162 --> 00:32:22,511
Notice the FFN keeps the default bias equals true, while our attention uses bias false.

370
00:32:22,511 --> 00:32:26,234
That's a simplification common in educational code.

371
00:32:26,234 --> 00:32:36,087
The original GPT two actually does have attention biases, and the book's chapter eleven weight loading code deals with that difference.

372
00:32:36,937 --> 00:32:38,290
Layer normalisation.

373
00:32:38,290 --> 00:32:51,623
For each token separately, compute the mean and variance across its seven sixty eight features, subtract the mean, divide by the standard deviation, then apply a learned scale gamma and shift beta.

374
00:32:51,623 --> 00:32:57,038
This keeps the activations in a healthy range as data flows through many layers.

375
00:32:57,038 --> 00:33:04,076
Note it normalises across features for each token, unlike batch norm, which normalises across the batch.

376
00:33:04,076 --> 00:33:10,100
Parameters: gamma and beta, seven sixty eight each, one thousand five hundred thirty six.

377
00:33:10,100 --> 00:33:23,501
One catch I hit: the book's printed snippet writes LayerNorm with a keyword d model equals seven sixty eight, and that raises a type error, because the parameter is actually called normalized shape.

378
00:33:23,501 --> 00:33:27,832
Just pass the number positionally, as the notebook's classes do.

379
00:33:28,682 --> 00:33:30,127
Residual connections.

380
00:33:30,127 --> 00:33:35,219
Instead of replacing x with attention of x, we add: x plus attention of x.

381
00:33:35,219 --> 00:33:42,512
The layer only has to learn a refinement, a delta, rather than rebuilding the representation from scratch.

382
00:33:42,512 --> 00:33:48,085
The book's piano analogy: each lesson adds improvements to what you already know.

383
00:33:48,085 --> 00:33:50,218
And there's a training benefit.

384
00:33:50,218 --> 00:33:58,956
The derivative of x plus f of x with respect to x includes a plain one from the skip path, so gradients can flow straight back.

385
00:33:58,956 --> 00:34:11,410
Without it, as in the book's example, if every layer scales the gradient by zero point eight, after twelve layers a gradient of zero point five shrinks to about zero point oh three.

386
00:34:11,410 --> 00:34:14,713
With the skip, there's always an unimpeded path.

387
00:34:14,713 --> 00:34:18,084
That's how GPT three can stack ninety six blocks.

388
00:34:18,934 --> 00:34:20,783
Where does layer norm go?

389
00:34:20,783 --> 00:34:26,181
The original 2017 paper used post norm: add the residual, then normalise.

390
00:34:26,181 --> 00:34:35,573
GPT two and most modern models use pre norm: normalise the input to the sub-layer, then add the result back to the untouched x.

391
00:34:35,573 --> 00:34:37,274
The difference matters.

392
00:34:37,274 --> 00:34:45,557
In pre norm, the residual stream from input to output is pure addition, it never passes through a normalisation.

393
00:34:45,557 --> 00:34:54,579
That clean highway makes deep networks train more stably and often without the learning rate warm up that post norm needs.

394
00:34:54,579 --> 00:34:55,984
We'll use pre norm.

395
00:34:56,834 --> 00:34:58,444
Here is the whole block.

396
00:34:58,444 --> 00:35:01,329
Input x, batch by seq by seven sixty eight.

397
00:35:01,329 --> 00:35:07,099
Layer norm one, then multi-head attention, then add the result back to the original x.

398
00:35:07,099 --> 00:35:14,076
That sum goes through layer norm two, then the feed forward network, and the result is added back again.

399
00:35:14,076 --> 00:35:16,759
The dashed lines are the residual skips.

400
00:35:16,759 --> 00:35:19,912
Two sub-layers, each wrapped in norm, then add.

401
00:35:19,912 --> 00:35:22,327
That's the entire transformer block.

402
00:35:23,177 --> 00:35:26,914
And the code, which reads exactly like the diagram.

403
00:35:26,914 --> 00:35:34,753
The constructor creates two layer norms, the multi-head attention, the feed forward network, and a dropout.

404
00:35:34,753 --> 00:35:42,593
In forward: normalise x with ln1, run attention with the mask, apply dropout to the output and add it to x.

405
00:35:42,593 --> 00:35:47,941
Then normalise with ln2, run the feed forward network, dropout, add to x.

406
00:35:47,941 --> 00:35:50,505
Return x and the attention weights.

407
00:35:50,505 --> 00:35:58,051
Two residual lines, that's the whole block in about ten lines, built from pieces we've already written.

408
00:35:58,902 --> 00:36:01,261
Running the block on random input.

409
00:36:01,261 --> 00:36:07,576
Shapes in and out are both two six seven sixty eight, attention weights two twelve six six.

410
00:36:07,576 --> 00:36:19,305
The notebook also prints means: input mean zero point oh one oh eight, output zero point oh one two two, and the average absolute difference is about zero point two six.

411
00:36:19,305 --> 00:36:27,494
That's the residual design in action: the output is the input plus a moderate refinement, not a completely new vector.

412
00:36:27,494 --> 00:36:31,728
Your exact numbers will differ, since the weights are random.

413
00:36:32,577 --> 00:36:34,916
How many parameters in one block?

414
00:36:34,916 --> 00:36:41,649
The QKV projection is seven sixty eight times two three oh four, one point seven seven million.

415
00:36:41,649 --> 00:36:45,334
The output projection, five hundred ninety thousand.

416
00:36:45,334 --> 00:36:50,153
fc1 and fc2 about two point three six million each including biases.

417
00:36:50,153 --> 00:36:53,271
Two layer norms, three thousand seventy two.

418
00:36:53,271 --> 00:37:01,917
Total seven million eighty four thousand eight hundred, and I confirmed it by summing the parameters of the actual module.

419
00:37:01,917 --> 00:37:05,177
Twelve blocks gives about eighty five million.

420
00:37:05,177 --> 00:37:13,681
GPT two small has about a hundred twenty four million in total, the rest mostly being the token and position embeddings.

421
00:37:14,531 --> 00:37:17,351
Let's trace it once more as a summary.

422
00:37:17,351 --> 00:37:19,727
Input two six seven sixty eight.

423
00:37:19,727 --> 00:37:20,543
Layer norm.

424
00:37:20,543 --> 00:37:28,634
Multi-head attention splits into two by twelve by six by sixty four, attends per head, and concatenates back.

425
00:37:28,634 --> 00:37:29,896
Add the residual.

426
00:37:29,896 --> 00:37:30,712
Layer norm.

427
00:37:30,712 --> 00:37:37,318
Feed forward expands to three oh seven two with GELU and comes back to seven sixty eight.

428
00:37:37,318 --> 00:37:38,580
Add the residual.

429
00:37:38,580 --> 00:37:39,990
Output, same shape.

430
00:37:39,990 --> 00:37:49,194
A useful mental model: attention is communication between tokens, the feed forward network is computation within each token.

431
00:37:49,194 --> 00:37:51,495
A block does one round of each.

432
00:37:52,346 --> 00:37:55,009
And here's a preview of chapter eleven.

433
00:37:55,009 --> 00:38:10,649
A GPT two model is the embedding layer, twelve transformer blocks in a module list, a final layer norm, and a language model head, a linear layer from seven sixty eight to fifty thousand two hundred fifty seven vocabulary logits.

434
00:38:10,649 --> 00:38:16,522
The forward pass embeds, loops through the blocks, normalises, and projects to logits.

435
00:38:16,522 --> 00:38:24,922
This is a condensed sketch from the book, and the mask is created from the sequence length in the real chapter eleven code.

436
00:38:24,922 --> 00:38:28,884
Everything inside the loop, you now understand completely.

437
00:38:29,733 --> 00:38:30,795
Section seven.

438
00:38:30,795 --> 00:38:32,464
Visualising attention.

439
00:38:33,314 --> 00:38:34,955
The visualisation helper.

440
00:38:34,955 --> 00:38:39,812
It takes the attention weights, a list of token strings, and a head index.

441
00:38:39,812 --> 00:38:44,406
It picks the first sentence and that head, giving a seq by seq matrix.

442
00:38:44,406 --> 00:38:49,854
Detach removes it from the autograd graph, cpu and numpy convert it for matplotlib.

443
00:38:49,854 --> 00:38:54,317
imshow draws it as a heatmap with values fixed between zero and one.

444
00:38:54,317 --> 00:39:02,456
Then it labels the axes: keys, attended to, along the bottom, queries, attending from, down the side, and adds a colour bar.

445
00:39:03,306 --> 00:39:06,141
Then the notebook runs a real sentence.

446
00:39:06,141 --> 00:39:12,612
The GPT two tokenizer turns the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog into nine tokens.

447
00:39:12,612 --> 00:39:19,301
Notice the leading spaces, GPT two's tokenizer attaches the space to the start of each word.

448
00:39:19,301 --> 00:39:29,553
A fresh, untrained embedding layer and a fresh transformer block process them, and we get attention weights of one by twelve by nine by nine.

449
00:39:29,553 --> 00:39:33,624
Then it plots the first four heads in a two by two grid.

450
00:39:33,624 --> 00:39:41,913
Important: this block is untrained, so the patterns will be the causal triangle with fairly random weights inside.

451
00:39:42,763 --> 00:39:46,316
Here's the figure the notebook produced on my machine.

452
00:39:46,316 --> 00:39:51,974
You can see the causal structure: everything above the diagonal is dark, exactly zero.

453
00:39:51,974 --> 00:39:57,567
The first token always puts all its weight on itself, so the top left cell is bright.

454
00:39:57,567 --> 00:40:03,818
But inside the triangle, the patterns are essentially random, because nothing has been trained.

455
00:40:03,818 --> 00:40:06,976
That's still useful: it verifies the mask works.

456
00:40:07,826 --> 00:40:17,873
To see what trained heads look like, I loaded pretrained GPT two and plotted three heads for the sentence, the cat sat on the mat because it was tired.

457
00:40:17,873 --> 00:40:22,929
Layer zero, head one, attends mostly to the token itself, a bright diagonal.

458
00:40:22,929 --> 00:40:31,046
Layer four, head eleven, is a previous token head: each token looks at the one right before it, a diagonal shifted by one.

459
00:40:31,046 --> 00:40:36,436
And layer five, head one, dumps nearly all its attention on the very first token.

460
00:40:36,436 --> 00:40:44,420
You might hope the word it would attend to cat, but in this head, it puts zero point nine eight on the first token, the.

461
00:40:44,420 --> 00:40:52,137
That's a well known pattern often called an attention sink, where the first token acts as a place to park attention.

462
00:40:52,137 --> 00:40:54,732
Which leads directly to the next slide.

463
00:40:55,582 --> 00:40:58,642
The book is careful here, and so should we be.

464
00:40:58,642 --> 00:41:06,226
From heatmaps you can conclude that masking works, and that heads behave differently, some local, some long range.

465
00:41:06,226 --> 00:41:13,277
You cannot conclude that a head encodes syntax or semantics, or that attention weights explain a decision.

466
00:41:13,277 --> 00:41:26,449
The feed forward layers, residual stream and norms carry a lot of the computation, heads interact with each other, and a weight tells you where a head reads from, not what it does with what it read.

467
00:41:26,449 --> 00:41:32,037
The attention sink we just saw is a perfect example: high weight, almost no meaning.

468
00:41:32,037 --> 00:41:35,363
Use visualisations for intuition, not explanation.

469
00:41:36,213 --> 00:41:37,231
Section eight.

470
00:41:37,231 --> 00:41:39,050
Practical considerations.

471
00:41:39,900 --> 00:41:48,862
Attention's big cost is memory that grows with the square of the sequence length, because the score tensor is batch by heads by n by n.

472
00:41:48,862 --> 00:41:54,106
At n equals five twelve, that's two hundred sixty two thousand scores per head.

473
00:41:54,106 --> 00:41:57,292
At two thousand forty eight, about four million.

474
00:41:57,292 --> 00:42:14,220
With a batch of eight and twelve heads at two thousand forty eight tokens, that's eight times twelve times two thousand forty eight squared, about four hundred million floats, times four bytes, roughly one point six gigabytes, for just one layer's scores.

475
00:42:14,220 --> 00:42:16,942
Double the context, quadruple the memory.

476
00:42:16,942 --> 00:42:19,331
GPT two used ten twenty four tokens.

477
00:42:19,331 --> 00:42:25,505
Longer contexts need more efficient techniques, which the book returns to in chapter sixteen.

478
00:42:26,355 --> 00:42:30,070
The notebook ends by running everything end to end.

479
00:42:30,070 --> 00:42:42,456
Tokenise the cat sat on the mat into six IDs, embed them to one by six by seven sixty eight, build a causal mask of size six, and push them through one transformer block.

480
00:42:42,456 --> 00:42:46,973
Output one by six by seven sixty eight, context aware vectors.

481
00:42:46,973 --> 00:42:57,536
In my run the token IDs were four sixty four, three seven nine seven, three three three two, three nineteen, two sixty two, and two six oh three.

482
00:42:57,536 --> 00:43:08,610
Small detail: the book's text lists mat as two one five four seven, but the real GPT two tokenizer gives two six oh three, so trust the notebook output.

483
00:43:09,460 --> 00:43:11,636
Let's wrap up with the key takeaways.

484
00:43:12,486 --> 00:43:13,774
What did we build?

485
00:43:13,774 --> 00:43:19,354
Scaled dot product attention, softmax of Q K transpose over root d k, times V.

486
00:43:19,354 --> 00:43:23,361
Causal masking with tril and masked fill minus infinity.

487
00:43:23,361 --> 00:43:30,945
Multi-head attention, twelve heads of sixty four, one fused projection, and the reshape and permute trick.

488
00:43:30,945 --> 00:43:41,891
A complete pre norm transformer block with attention, a GELU feed forward network and two residual connections, about seven point one million parameters.

489
00:43:41,891 --> 00:43:49,045
And attention heatmaps, which are great for checking the mask and dangerous for over-interpretation.

490
00:43:49,895 --> 00:43:51,221
The core concepts.

491
00:43:51,221 --> 00:43:56,153
Static embeddings become context aware vectors with the same shape.

492
00:43:56,153 --> 00:44:00,937
Query is what I seek, key is how I'm found, value is what I give.

493
00:44:00,937 --> 00:44:06,385
We scale by root d k because the dot product's spread grows like root d k.

494
00:44:06,385 --> 00:44:12,053
Multi-head attention gives different perspectives at no extra parameter cost.

495
00:44:12,053 --> 00:44:15,881
Residuals and layer norm make deep stacks trainable.

496
00:44:15,881 --> 00:44:25,303
And this exact mechanism powers GPT four, BERT, vision transformers and even AlphaFold, applied to different kinds of sequences.

497
00:44:26,153 --> 00:44:27,333
Review questions.

498
00:44:27,333 --> 00:44:29,970
Pause and answer each one before I do.

499
00:44:29,970 --> 00:44:33,440
Question one: why aren't static embeddings enough?

500
00:44:33,440 --> 00:44:37,813
Because bank gets the same vector in river bank and money bank.

501
00:44:37,813 --> 00:44:45,586
The lookup can't see neighbours, attention mixes in context so the vectors diverge, as we saw with real GPT two.

502
00:44:45,586 --> 00:44:51,277
Question two: the roles of Q, K and V, and why the dot product measures relevance.

503
00:44:51,277 --> 00:44:57,870
The query is what a token seeks, the key is what it advertises, the value is what it passes on.

504
00:44:57,870 --> 00:45:02,243
The dot product is large when query and key point the same way.

505
00:45:02,243 --> 00:45:04,880
Question three: why scale by root d k?

506
00:45:04,880 --> 00:45:11,335
Because the dot product's standard deviation grows like root d k, about eight for sixty four.

507
00:45:11,335 --> 00:45:17,859
Without scaling softmax saturates, the weights become nearly one hot and the gradients vanish.

508
00:45:18,709 --> 00:45:21,254
Question four: what is causal masking?

509
00:45:21,254 --> 00:45:27,682
A lower triangular mask that sets future scores to minus infinity, so their weights become zero.

510
00:45:27,682 --> 00:45:32,571
That makes training match generation, where the future doesn't exist yet.

511
00:45:32,571 --> 00:45:38,531
Question five: how do multiple heads give different perspectives without more parameters?

512
00:45:38,531 --> 00:45:44,759
We split seven sixty eight into twelve heads of sixty four, which is still seven sixty eight.

513
00:45:44,759 --> 00:45:49,380
The fused projection is still seven sixty eight by two three oh four.

514
00:45:49,380 --> 00:45:53,465
Each head just gets its own slice and learns its own pattern.

515
00:45:53,465 --> 00:45:55,608
Question six: the reshape trick.

516
00:45:55,608 --> 00:46:09,135
We reshape to separate Q, K, V and heads, then permute so heads act like a batch dimension, and one batched matrix multiply handles all heads at once on optimised kernels, instead of a slow Python loop.

517
00:46:09,985 --> 00:46:13,452
Question seven: the four components of a block.

518
00:46:13,452 --> 00:46:17,361
Attention, for exchanging information between tokens.

519
00:46:17,361 --> 00:46:22,524
The feed forward network, for non-linear processing within each token.

520
00:46:22,524 --> 00:46:28,868
Residual connections, which preserve the original signal and give gradients a highway.

521
00:46:28,868 --> 00:46:33,146
And layer norm, which keeps activations in a stable range.

522
00:46:33,146 --> 00:46:36,244
Question eight: pre norm versus post norm.

523
00:46:36,244 --> 00:46:41,186
Post norm is layer norm of x plus f of x, as in the original paper.

524
00:46:41,186 --> 00:46:51,365
Pre norm is x plus f of layer norm of x, used by GPT two, because the residual path stays pure addition and deep models train more stably.

525
00:46:52,215 --> 00:46:57,911
Question nine: memory for batch four, twelve heads, sequence two thousand forty eight.

526
00:46:57,911 --> 00:47:04,334
Four times twelve times two thousand forty eight squared is about two hundred one million floats.

527
00:47:04,334 --> 00:47:09,831
At four bytes each, that's about zero point eight gigabytes for one layer's scores.

528
00:47:09,831 --> 00:47:12,479
Double the length and memory quadruples.

529
00:47:12,479 --> 00:47:15,393
Question ten: why be cautious with heatmaps?

530
00:47:15,393 --> 00:47:18,770
They show where a head reads, not what it computes.

531
00:47:18,770 --> 00:47:26,585
Feed forward layers and residuals matter too, heads interact, and attention sinks get huge weight with little meaning.

532
00:47:26,585 --> 00:47:29,366
You can confirm masking and see diversity.

533
00:47:29,366 --> 00:47:34,001
You can't claim a head does syntax or that weights explain a decision.

534
00:47:34,851 --> 00:47:38,377
Your practice list, all in the chapter ten notebook.

535
